1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:04,752
The Stafford gambit gives up a pawn for rapid threats.

2
00:00:04,912 --> 00:00:09,365
Both knights enter before either king can relax.

3
00:00:09,525 --> 00:00:13,977
White wins the first knight, but the center opens.

4
00:00:14,137 --> 00:00:18,590
The recapture rebuilds Black's pressure on the dark squares.

5
00:00:18,750 --> 00:00:23,203
White pushes again and attacks the exposed knight.

6
00:00:23,363 --> 00:00:27,815
Black ignores the attack and strikes at f two.

7
00:00:27,975 --> 00:00:32,428
The knight takes with check-like pressure on the king.

8
00:00:32,587 --> 00:00:37,040
White's king captures, but now it cannot castle.

9
00:00:37,200 --> 00:00:41,652
The bishop lands on c five with check.

10
00:00:41,812 --> 00:00:46,265
After Be three, the queen check keeps the king in the open.

11
00:00:46,425 --> 00:00:50,877
The lesson: a material win can cost more than one tempo.

12
00:00:51,037 --> 00:00:55,650
Would you accept the Stafford gambit?

